g works as expected. chunk 2 = data[12:18] = \"7\\n8\\n9\\n\" ✓\n\nNow testing with generateLines(1, 9) confirms the algorithm distributes chunks evenly across lines of equal length, with each chunk containing complete lines.\n\nFor the round-robin distribution, my implementation matches GNU's behavior exactly — cycling through files in order. Let me verify the byte-offset calculation one more time to ensure it handles the chunk boundaries correctly. for empty input with `-b`. ✓\n\nI'm ready to start implementing. One thing I should verify—do the conflict tests need the `LC_ALL=C` environment variable that the summary mentions for differential tests? Looking at the existing test patterns, they don't set `Env`, so I'll skip it for now and add it if needed. The test file already has `bytes` imported, so that's covered.\n\nNow I'm setting up the test functions...\n\nWriting test cases... Defining more test cases... Defining test cases for chunking and conflict handling...\n\nI'm noticing a potential issue with the conflict tests—our binary outputs `split: cannot split in more than one way` but GNU split uses `gsplit:` as the prefix, so the stderr comparison in RunDiffTests might fail since the error messages won't match exactly.\n\nLooking at the DiffTest struct, I don't see an `ExpectedStderr` field, so stderr probably isn't being compared at all—just exit codes and output files. I'll proceed with this assumption and adjust if tests fail.\n\nFor the byte chunk test with \"abcdefghi\" (9 bytes) split into 3 chunks, that's 3 bytes per chunk cleanly. The line test with 9 lines of 2 bytes each (18 total) split into 3 chunks gives 6 bytes per chunk, which aligns perfectly with line boundaries so each chunk gets exactly 3 lines.\n\nNow I'm realizing the chunk byte test might have an issue—GNU split with `-n` mode typically requires seekable input, but my test is using stdin. Let me verify whether GNU split actually allows `-n` with stdin or if it throws an error. I'm realizing that GNU coreutils handles stdin with `-n` by buffering it to a temp file internally, so the differential test should reveal any discrepancies between my byte-chunking logic and GNU split's output. I'm confident the implementation handles the distribution correctly, including edge cases like empty input or uneven divisions. Now I'm going to make the edits, but I should also consider how `splitLineChunks` works with...\n\nActually, let me trace through what happens when there's no trailing newline. If I have \"abc\" split into 2 chunks, the first chunk captures the entire line since there's no newline to stop it, and the second chunk ends up empty—which matches what GNU split does. I'm confident the logic is correct across both implementations. Now I'm writing the actual code—a function that splits the data into n chunks by finding line boundaries, calculating target positions proportionally, and writing each piece to a file.\n\nDefining the line-finding helper...\n\nNow I need to think through the round-robin distribution more carefully, especially how GNU split handles the `/N` syntax—it creates all N files regardless of whether some end up empty.\n\nTesting with 2 lines across 5 files, the first line goes to file 0, the second to file 1, and files 2-4 remain empty. My implementation distributes lines cyclically across N buffers, then writes each one out, so the empty buffers naturally produce zero-length files when written.\n\nI'm verifying that `bytes.Buffer.Bytes()` returns `nil` for empty buffers, and since `writePiece` checks `len(data) > 0` before writing, it just creates the file via `os.Create` and closes it without writing anything. Now I need to confirm whether GNU split actually creates these empty files in r/N mode or if it skips them entirely.\n\nLooking at the GNU source, r/N mode creates files lazily—only when lines are actually assigned to a chunk, so empty files shouldn't exist. I need to adjust my implementation to skip writing files that have