{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013LxyEnzWk2aDtqZ3CYsSVk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Three failures:\n\n1. **line_bytes_long_line**: Expected \"abcdefghij\\n\" but got \"abcde\". The issue is that my implementation reads lines using `br.ReadBytes('\\n')`, but for `-C 5`, the actual gsplit behavior differs. With `--line-bytes=5`, it splits by bytes but tries to break at line boundaries. For a line longer than N bytes, gsplit seems to write N bytes at a time even mid-line, not the entire line.\n\nWait, let me re-read R2.2: \"-C N or --line-bytes=N must split the input into pieces of at most N bytes each, breaking only at line boundaries. \n\nLines longer than N bytes are written to their own piece.\" But the actual behavior from gsplit is different—for lines longer than N bytes, it writes exactly N bytes per piece. So \"abcdefghij\\n\" (11 bytes) with -C 5 would be split into \"abcde\" (5 bytes), \"fghij\" (5 bytes), \"\\n\" (1 byte), not kept as one piece. The test failure shows gsplit produced \"abcde\" as the first piece, which means it's breaking long lines at byte boundaries rather than preserving them intact.\n\nI need to rethink my `splitLineBytes` function. The correct behavior is to read data and break at line boundaries when possible, but if a line exceeds N bytes, split it into N-byte chunks instead of keeping it whole. So I need to handle both cases: lines that fit within the byte limit go into the current piece if there's room, otherwise start a new piece, and lines that exceed the limit get broken into multiple N-byte pieces. I need to trace through the actual behavior more carefully with a concrete example to understand how `-C` handles line breaks and chunk boundaries. I think I've been overcomplicating this. The `-C N` algorithm actually looks back from position N to find the last newline within those N bytes, then writes up to and including that newline if found, or